    "questionText": "CALCULATOR FREE 10 MATHEMATICS METHODS UNIT 1 Question 8 {1.1.19} (5 marks) Let 𝑃(𝑥) = 𝑥3 + 𝑘𝑥2 −(𝑘+ 3)𝑥−2𝑘−2 (a) Show that 𝑃(𝑥) is divisible by 𝑥+ 1. (1 mark) (b) Factorise 𝑃(𝑥) fully. (4 marks) END OF SECTION ONE CALCULATOR FREE 11 MATHEMATICS METHODS UNIT 1 Additional working space Question number: CALCULATOR FREE 12 MATHEMATICS METHODS UNIT 1 Additional working space Question number:",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR FREE 10 MATHEMATICS METHODS UNIT 1 Question 8 {1.1.19} (5 marks) Let 𝑃(𝑥) = 𝑥3 + 𝑘𝑥2 −(𝑘+ 3)𝑥−2𝑘−2 (a) Show that 𝑃(𝑥) is divisible by 𝑥+ 1. (1 mark) Solution 𝑃(𝑥) = (−1) + 𝑘+ 𝑘+ 3 −2𝑘−2 𝑃(𝑥) = 0− ) + 𝑘+ 𝑘+ 3 −2𝑘−2 By the Factor Theorem, 𝑃(𝑥) is divisible by 𝑥+ 1. Specific behaviours ✓ uses the Factor Theorem (b) Factorise 𝑃(𝑥) fully. (4 marks) Solution 𝑥2 + (𝑘−1)𝑥−2𝑘−2 𝑥+ 1⟌ 𝑥3 + 𝑘𝑥2 −(𝑘+ 3)𝑥−2𝑘−2 ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅ −) 𝑥3 + 𝑥 (𝑘−1)𝑥2 −(𝑘+ 3)𝑥 −) (𝑘−1)𝑥2 + (𝑘−1)𝑥 (−2𝑘−2)𝑥−2𝑘−2 −) (−2𝑘−2)𝑥−2𝑘−2 0 0 ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅ 𝑃(𝑥) = (𝑥+ 1)[𝑥2 + (𝑘−1)𝑥−2(𝑘+ 1)] = (𝑥+ 1)(𝑥−2)(𝑥+ 𝑘+ 1) Specific behaviours ✓ beginning a division or comparing coefficients ✓ finding 𝑎= 1 ✓ finding the correct quotient in full ✓ factorising the quotient END OF SECTION ONE",
