    "topicCode": "1.1",
    "topicName": "Counting & Probability",
    "topicLabel": "Topic 1.1 · Counting & Probability",
    "calculator": "Unlabelled",
    "questionText": "Applecross SHS Semester 1, 2020 Section 2 Mathematics Methods Unit 1&2 8 See next page DO NOT WRITE IN THIS SECTION AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF. Question 14 (6 marks) When a random sample of 173 people from a university were classified according to whether they had a driver's licence (event 𝐷) and whether they wore spectacles (event 𝑆), it was observed that 𝑛(𝐷) = 140, 𝑛(𝑆) = 53 and 𝑛(𝑆∩𝐷̅) = 10. (a) Determine (i) 𝑛(𝑆̅). (1 mark) (ii) 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆). (1 mark) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen person from the sample (i) does not have a driver's licence. (2 marks) (ii) wears spectacles given that they have a driver's licence. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN002-152-4 Question 14 (6 marks) When a random sample of 173 people from a university were classified according to whether they had a driver's licence (event 𝐷) and whether they wore spectacles (event 𝑆), it was observed that 𝑛(𝐷) = 140, 𝑛(𝑆) = 53 and 𝑛(𝑆∩𝐷̅) = 10. (a) Determine (i) 𝑛(𝑆̅). (1 mark) (ii) 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆). (1 mark) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen person from the sample (i) does not have a driver's licence. (2 marks) (ii) wears spectacles given that they have a driver's licence. (2 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝑆|𝐷) = 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆) 𝑛(𝐷) = 43 140 ≈0.307 Specific behaviours ✓ numerator ✓ denominator Solution 𝑛(𝑆̅) = 173 −53 = 120 Specific behaviours ✓ correct number Solution 𝑛(𝐷̅) = 173 −140 = 33 𝑃(𝐷̅) = 33 173 ≈0.191 Specific behaviours ✓ numerator ✓ denominator Solution 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆) = 53 −10 = 43 Specific behaviours ✓ correct number",
