    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN044-132-3 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 14 (8 marks) Two events, 𝐴 and 𝐵, have probabilities 𝑃(𝐴) = 0.4 and 𝑃(𝐵) = 0.65. (a) Determine 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) in each of the following cases: (i) 𝐴 and 𝐵 are independent. (1 mark) (ii) 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 0.8. (2 marks) (iii) 𝑃(𝐴|(𝐴∪𝐵)) = 4 9. (3 marks) (b) Is it possible that 𝐴 and 𝐵 are mutually exclusive events? Explain your answer. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN044-132-4 Question 14 (8 marks) Two events, 𝐴 and 𝐵, have probabilities 𝑃(𝐴) = 0.4 and 𝑃(𝐵) = 0.65. (a) Determine 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) in each of the following cases: (i) 𝐴 and 𝐵 are independent. (1 mark) (ii) 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 0.8. (2 marks) (iii) 𝑃(𝐴|(𝐴∪𝐵)) = 4 9. (3 marks) (b) Is it possible that 𝐴 and 𝐵 are mutually exclusive events? Explain your answer. (2 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝐴) × 𝑃(𝐵) = 0.4 × 0.65 = 0.26 = 13 50 Specific behaviours ✓ correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 𝑃(𝐴) + 𝑃(𝐵) −𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 0.4 + 0.65 −0.8 = 0.25 = 1 4 Specific behaviours ✓ indicates use of appropriate rule ✓ correct probability Solution No. Since 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 0 for mutually exclusive events, then 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 𝑃(𝐴) + 𝑃(𝐵) = 1.05 > 1 - impossible. Specific behaviours ✓ states no ✓ explains using 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 0 Solution 𝑥= 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 0.4 −𝑥+ 0.65 −𝑥= 1.05 −2𝑥 𝑃(𝐴|(𝐴∪𝐵)) = 0.4 1.05 −𝑥= 4 9 𝑥= 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 0.15 = 3 20 Specific behaviours ✓ indicates use of appropriate rules ✓ forms equation ✓ correct probability",
