    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN044-152-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 14 (9 marks) When a random sample of 173 people from a university were classified according to whether they had a driver's licence (event 𝐷) and whether they wore spectacles (event 𝑆), it was observed that 𝑛(𝐷) = 140, 𝑛(𝑆) = 53 and 𝑛(𝑆∩𝐷̅) = 10. (a) Determine (i) 𝑛(𝑆̅). (1 mark) (ii) 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆). (1 mark) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen person from the sample (i) does not have a driver's licence. (2 marks) (ii) wears spectacles given that they have a driver's licence. (2 marks) CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 9 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN044-152-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF (c) Does the sample provide any indication of possible independence of events 𝑆 and 𝐷? Justify your answer. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN044-152-4 Question 14 (9 marks) When a random sample of 173 people from a university were classified according to whether they had a driver's licence (event 𝐷) and whether they wore spectacles (event 𝑆), it was observed that 𝑛(𝐷) = 140, 𝑛(𝑆) = 53 and 𝑛(𝑆∩𝐷̅) = 10. (a) Determine (i) 𝑛(𝑆̅). (1 mark) (ii) 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆). (1 mark) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen person from the sample (i) does not have a driver's licence. (2 marks) (ii) wears spectacles given that they have a driver's licence. (2 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝑆|𝐷) = 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆) 𝑛(𝐷) = 43 140 ≈0.307 Specific behaviours ✓ numerator ✓ denominator Solution 𝑛(𝑆̅) = 173 −53 = 120 Specific behaviours ✓ correct number Solution 𝑛(𝐷̅) = 173 −140 = 33 𝑃(𝐷̅) = 33 173 ≈0.191 Specific behaviours ✓ numerator ✓ denominator Solution 𝑛(𝐷∩𝑆) = 53 −10 = 43 Specific behaviours ✓ correct number",
