    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN044-152-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 18 (9 marks) The probabilities that Alf, Bess and Cleo will attend the next work social are 𝑃(𝐴) = 0.7, 𝑃(𝐵) = 0.55 and 𝑃(𝐶) = 0.85 respectively. It is also known that 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 0.45. (a) Determine 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵). (2 marks) (b) Describe, in the context of this question, the event (𝐴∩𝐵̅) ∪(𝐴̅ ∩𝐵) and calculate the probability that it happens. (3 marks) (c) State, with justification, whether events 𝐴 and 𝐵 are mutually exclusive. (1 mark) CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 15 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN044-152-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF (d) The attendance of Cleo at the next work social is independent of the attendance of anyone else. Determine the probability that none of the three named people attend the next work social. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN044-152-4 Question 18 (9 marks) The probabilities that Alf, Bess and Cleo will attend the next work social are 𝑃(𝐴) = 0.7, 𝑃(𝐵) = 0.55 and 𝑃(𝐶) = 0.85 respectively. It is also known that 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 0.45. (a) Determine 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵). (2 marks) (b) Describe, in the context of this question, the event (𝐴∩𝐵̅) ∪(𝐴̅ ∩𝐵) and calculate the probability that it happens. (3 marks) (c) State, with justification, whether events 𝐴 and 𝐵 are mutually exclusive. (1 mark) Solution No, since 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) ≠0. Specific behaviours ✓ uses probability rule for ME events Solution The event means that either Alf or Bess but not both attend the next social. 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵̅) = 0.7 −0.45 = 0.25 𝑃(𝐴̅ ∩𝐵) = 0.55 −0.45 = 0.1 𝑃= 0.25 + 0.1 = 0.35 Specific behaviours ✓ description ✓ one correct part probability ✓ correct answer Solution 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 0.7 + 0.55 −0.45 = 0.8 Specific behaviours ✓ uses probability rule ✓ correct probability",
