    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 18 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED End of questions SN044-152-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 21 (8 marks) A squad of 6 cyclists is to be chosen at random from 17 applicants. 3 of applicants live in Tasmania, 6 live in WA and the rest live in Queensland. (a) Determine the number of different squads that can be chosen. (2 marks) (b) Determine the number of different squads that can be chosen that (i) include all the Tasmanians. (2 marks) (ii) include an equal number of cyclists from each of the states. (2 marks) (iii) have at least 5 cyclists from Queensland. (2 marks) © 2020 WA Exam Papers. John Wollaston Anglican Community School has a non- exclusive licence to copy and communicate this document for non-commercial, educational use within the school. No other copying, communication or use is permitted without the express written permission of WA Exam Papers. SN044-152-2.",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 18 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED End of questions SN044-152-4 Question 21 (8 marks) A squad of 6 cyclists is to be chosen at random from 17 applicants. 3 of applicants live in Tasmania, 6 live in WA and the rest live in Queensland. (a) Determine the number of different squads that can be chosen. (2 marks) (b) Determine the number of different squads that can be chosen that (i) include all the Tasmanians. (2 marks) (ii) include an equal number of cyclists from each of the states. (2 marks) (iii) have at least 5 cyclists from Queensland. (2 marks) Solution (17 6 ) = 12 376 Specific behaviours ✓ indicates use of combination formula ✓ correct number Solution (3 2) (6 2) (8 2) = 3 × 15 × 28 = 1 260 Specific behaviours ✓ indicates correct method ✓ correct number Solution (3 3) (14 3 ) = 1 × 364 = 364 Specific behaviours ✓ indicates correct method ✓ correct number Solution (8 5) (9 1) + (8 6) (9 0) = 56 × 9 + 28 × 1 = 504 + 28 = 532 Specific behaviours ✓ indicates correct method ✓ correct number",
