    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "CALCULATOR ASSUMED 5 MATHEMATICS METHODS UNIT 1 Question 10 {1.3.13, 1.3.15, 1.3.16} (8 marks) In a school survey of 207 students in Year 11 and Year 12, it was observed that 77 of the 102 Year 12 students studied Mathematics and the 10 students in Year 11 did not study Mathematics. If one student is selected at random from those surveyed, determine the probability that (a) they were in Year 11. (1 mark) (b) they studied Mathematics. (1 mark) (c) they were in Year 12 or studied Mathematics. (2 marks) (d) They studied Mathematics given they were in Year 11. (2 marks) (e) Without calculating any further probabilities, is there any indication that studying Mathematics is independent of Year? Justify your answer. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR ASSUMED 5 MATHEMATICS METHODS UNIT 1 Question 10 {1.3.13, 1.3.15, 1.3.16} (8 marks) In a school survey of 207 students in Year 11 and Year 12, it was observed that 77 of the 102 Year 12 students studied Mathematics and the 10 students in Year 11 did not study Mathematics. If one student is selected at random from those surveyed, determine the probability that (a) they were in Year 11. (1 mark) Solution 𝑃(𝑌𝑒𝑎𝑟 11) = 207 −102 207 = 105 207 Specific behaviours ✓ correct probability (b) they studied Mathematics. (1 mark) Solution 𝑃(𝑀𝑎𝑡ℎ𝑠) = 77 + (105 −10) 207 = 172 207 Specific behaviours ✓ correct probability (c) they were in Year 12 or studied Mathematics. (2 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝑌𝑒𝑎𝑟 12 ∪𝑀𝑎𝑡ℎ𝑠) = 102 + 172 −77 207 = 197 207 Specific behaviours ✓ uses inclusion-exclusion principle ✓ correct probability (d) They studied Mathematics given they were in Year 11. (2 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝑀𝑎𝑡ℎ𝑠|𝑌𝑒𝑎𝑟 11) = 95 105 Specific behaviours ✓ correct numerator ✓ correct denominator (e) Without calculating any further probabilities, is there any indication that studying Mathematics is independent of Year? Justify your answer. (2 marks) Solution The choice of Mathematics is NOT independent of yea",
