    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 12 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-112-2 Question 18 (9 marks) In a group of students, it is known that 70% are in Year 12 and the rest in Year 11. 40% of those in Year 12 and 80% of those in Year 11 study maths, while the remainder do not. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram to represent the above information. Start Y 12 Y 11 0.7 (2 marks) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student from the group (i) is in Year 11 and studies maths. (1 mark) (ii) studies maths. (2 marks) (iii) is in Year 11, given that they study maths. (2 marks) (iv) is in Year 12 or studies maths. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 12 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-112-4 Question 18 (9 marks) In a group of 250 students, it is known that 70% are in Year 12 and the rest in Year 11. 40% of those in Year 12 and 80% of those in Year 11 study maths, while the remainder do not. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram to represent the above information. Start Y 12 Y 11 0.7 (2 marks) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student from the group (i) is in Year 11 and studies math. (1 mark) (ii) studies math. (2 marks) (iii) is in Year 11, given that they study math. (2 marks) (iv) is in Year 12 or studies math. (2 marks) Specific behaviours  all correct -1 per omission Solution 𝑃(𝑇̅ ∩𝑀) = 0.3 × 0.8 = 0.24 (≡6 25) Specific behaviours  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑇∩𝑀) + 𝑃(𝑇̅ ∩𝑀) = 0.7 × 0.4 + 0.24 = 0.28 + 0.24 = 0.52 (≡13 25) Specific behaviours  indicates suitable method  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑇̅|𝑀) = 0.24 0.52 = 6 13 (≈0.4615) Specific behaviours  uses (i) and (ii)  expresses 𝑃 correctly (no need to fully simplify) Solution 𝑃(𝑇) + 𝑃(𝑀) −𝑃(𝑇∩𝑀) = 0.7 + 0.52 −0.28 = 0.94 (≡47 50) Specific behaviours  indicates suitable method  correct probability",
