    "calculator": "Unlabelled",
    "questionText": "See next page Question 23 (10 marks) The manager of a private bus company has worked out a model for the relationship between the number of passengers carried each week and the profit, (in tens of $), the company makes. If n is the number of passengers carried, the profit is given by . (a) Can the relation P(n) be described as a function. Justify your answer. (1 mark) (b) Determine the minimum number of passengers needed per week for the company to make a profit. (1 mark) (c) Determine the maximum profit made according to this model and how many passengers are carried during the week to achieve this profit. (2 marks) See next page (d) (i) Determine the simplified expression for P(n + 1)  P(n), and explain what this expression represents. (4 marks) (ii) How many passengers would the bus company carry in a week if the profit made by one extra passenger was 50 cents? (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "See next page Question 23 (9 marks) The manager of a private bus company has worked out a model for the relationship between the number of passengers carried each week and the profit, (in tens of $), the company makes. If n is the number of passengers carried, the profit is given by . (a) Can the relation P(n) be described as a function. Justify your answer. (1 mark) (b) Determine the minimum number of passengers needed per week for the company to make a profit. (1 mark) (c) Determine the maximum profit made according to this model and how many passengers are carried during the week to achieve this profit. (2 marks) Solution Yes, every value of n has only one value of P. (one-to-one mapping) Or as a quadratic – it is a function Specific behaviours   correct answer with justification Solution Solve P(n)=0 CAS soln = 1055.7 N=1056 passengers to make a profit Specific behaviours   correct answer Solution Coordinates of Max TP ( 10000, 8000) CAS 10000 passengers and $80 000 profit for that week Specific behaviours   correct number of passengers   correct profit in dollars",
