    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-192-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 10 (8 marks) Triangle 𝐴𝐵𝐶 has sides 𝑎= 17.2 cm, 𝑐= 21.6 cm and an area of 171 cm2. (a) Determine all possible values for the size of angle 𝐵, in degrees. (3 marks) (b) Determine, showing use of trigonometry, the largest possible perimeter of triangle 𝐴𝐵𝐶. (3 marks) (c) Determine, showing use of trigonometry, the size of angle 𝐴 when triangle 𝐴𝐵𝐶 has the largest possible perimeter. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-192-4 Question 10 (8 marks) Triangle 𝐴𝐵𝐶 has sides 𝑎= 17.2 cm, 𝑐= 21.6 cm and an area of 171 cm2. (a) Determine all possible values for the size of angle 𝐵 in degrees. (3 marks) (b) Determine, showing use of trigonometry, the largest possible perimeter of triangle 𝐴𝐵𝐶. (3 marks) (c) Determine, showing use of trigonometry, the size of angle 𝐴 when triangle 𝐴𝐵𝐶 has the largest possible perimeter. (2 marks) Solution 𝑏2 = 17.22 + 21.62 −2(17.2)(21.6) cos 113° 𝑏= 32.4 𝑃= 17.2 + 21.6 + 32.4 = 71.2 cm Specific behaviours  shows use of trigonometry  correct length of b  correct perimeter with units If used 67° then b=21.7 and P 60.5 cm max 2/3 marks Solution 171 = 1 2(17.2)(21.6) sin 𝐵 sin 𝐵= 0.92054, 0° ≤𝐵≤180° 𝐵= 67.0° or 113.0° Specific behaviours  substitutes into area formula correctly  solves for one correct angle  solves for both correct angles Allow any degree of accuracy Solution sin𝐴 17.2 = sin 113° 32.4 𝐴= 29.2° Specific behaviours  shows use of trigonometry  solves for correct angle (no marks for considering ambiguous case as already awarded in (a) ) If used 67° then A=47° allow fullmarks follow through",
