    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 11 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-192-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 16 (8 marks) (a) The graph of the quadratic function 𝑦= 𝑓(𝑥) has roots at 𝑥= −7 and 𝑥= 3 and the range of 𝑓(𝑥) is 𝑦≤50. Use an algebraic method to determine 𝑓(0). (4 marks) (b) The area of square 𝐵 is 655.5 cm2 more than six times the area of square 𝐴, and the difference in the perimeters of the two squares is 98 cm. Determine the least possible area of square 𝐴, the smaller of the squares. (4 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 11 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-192-4 Question 16 (8 marks) (a) The graph of the quadratic function 𝑦= 𝑓(𝑥) has roots at 𝑥= −7 and 𝑥= 3 and the range of 𝑓(𝑥) is 𝑦≤50. Use an algebraic method to determine 𝑓(0). (4 marks) (b) The area of square 𝐵 is 655.5 cm2 more than six times the area of square 𝐴, and the difference in the perimeters of the two squares is 98 cm. Determine the least possible area of square 𝐴, the smaller of the squares. (4 marks) Solution Side of 𝐵 must be 98 ÷ 4 = 24.5 cm longer than side of 𝐴. If side of 𝐴 is 𝑥, then side of 𝐵 is 𝑥+ 24.5. Hence (𝑥+ 24.5)2 = 6𝑥2 + 655.5 𝑥= 13 10 = 1.3, 𝑥= 17 2 = 8.5 Least area when 𝑥= 1.3: 𝐴𝐴= 1.32 = 169 100 = 1.69 cm2 Specific behaviours  indicates difference in the side lengths is 24.5  forms correct quadratic equation  solves quadratic equation  states correct least area for simultaneous eqns: 1 mark each eqn, 1 mark for solve for x, 1 mark correct area Solution Axis of symmetry is midway between roots (−7 + 3) ÷ 2 = −2 and so turning point at (−2, 50). 𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑎(𝑥+ 2)2 + 50 Using a root, 𝑓(3) = 0 = 𝑎(3 + 2)2 + 50 →𝑎= −2. 𝑓(𝑥) = −2(3 + 2)2 + 50 Hence 𝑓(0) = −2(0 + 2)2 + 50 = 42. Specific behaviours ",
