    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-212-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 13 (9 marks) The graph of the hyperbola 𝑦= 𝑝 𝑥+ 𝑞 , where 𝑝 and 𝑞 are constants, passes through the point (7, 1) and intercepts the vertical axis at 𝑦= −4 3 ⁄ . (a) Show how to use this information to deduce that 𝑝= 4 and 𝑞= −3. (2 marks) (b) State the equations of the asymptotes of the hyperbola. (2 marks) The line with equation 𝑦= 16𝑥−48 intersects the hyperbola at two points. (c) Show how to use the equations of the line and the hyperbola to formulate a quadratic equation in the form 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, the solutions of which are the 𝑥-coordinates of the points of intersection. Do not determine these 𝑥-coordinates. (3 marks) Solution Using (7, 1) and (0, −4 3 ⁄ ): 1 = 𝑝 7 + 𝑞⇒𝑝= 𝑞+ 7, −4 3 = 𝑝 𝑞⇒𝑝= −4𝑞 3 Hence −4𝑞 3 = 𝑞+ 7 ⇒7𝑞 3 = −7 ⇒𝑞= −3, 𝑝= −3 + 7 = 4. Specific behaviours ✓ uses points to form two equations in 𝑝 and 𝑞 ✓ indicates appropriate solution method (CAS, algebraic, etc) Solution Using CAS to graph 𝑦= 4 𝑥−3 or by inspection, the equation of the horizontal asymptote is 𝑦= 0 and the equation of the vertical asymptote is 𝑥= 3. Specific behaviours ✓ equation",
