    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 15 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-212-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 18 (8 marks) When a chance experiment is carried out, events 𝑋 and 𝑌 occur with probabilities 0.75 and 0.52 respectively. (a) Explain why 𝑋 and 𝑌 cannot be mutually exclusive events. (2 marks) (b) Determine the following probabilities: (i) P( 𝑋∩𝑌) when P(𝑋∩𝑌 ) = 0.44. (2 marks) (ii) P(𝑋∪𝑌) when 𝑋 and 𝑌 are independent events. (2 marks) (iii) P( 𝑋∪𝑌 ) when P(𝑌 | 𝑋) = 0.54. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 15 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-212-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 18 (8 marks) When a chance experiment is carried out, events 𝑋 and 𝑌 occur with probabilities 0.75 and 0.52 respectively. (a) Explain why 𝑋 and 𝑌 cannot be mutually exclusive events. (2 marks) (b) Determine the following probabilities: (i) P( 𝑋∩𝑌) when P(𝑋∩𝑌 ) = 0.44. (2 marks) (ii) P(𝑋∪𝑌) when 𝑋 and 𝑌 are independent events. (2 marks) (iii) P( 𝑋∪𝑌 ) when P(𝑌 | 𝑋) = 0.54. (2 marks) Solution For mutually exclusive events, P(𝑋∪𝑌) = P(𝑋) + P(𝑌) = 0.75 + 0.52 = 1.27. This is clearly impossible as any probability must lie between 0 and 1. Specific behaviours ✓ shows P(𝑋) + P(𝑌) > 1 ✓ reasonable explanation Solution P(𝑋∩𝑌) = P(𝑋) −P(𝑋∩𝑌) = 0.75 −0.44 = 0.31 P( 𝑋∩𝑌) = P(𝑌) −P(𝑋∩𝑌) = 0.52 −0.31 = 0.21 Specific behaviours ✓ calculates P(𝑋∩𝑌) ✓ correct probability Solution For independent events, P(𝑋∩𝑌) = P(𝑋) × P(𝑌) = 0.75 × 0.52 = 0.39. Hence P(𝑋∪𝑌) = P(𝑋) + P(𝑌) −P(𝑋∩𝑌) = 0.75 + 0.52 −0.39 = 0.88. Specific behaviours ✓ calculates P(𝑋∩𝑌) ✓ correct probability Solution P(𝑌 | 𝑋) = P(𝑋∩𝑌) P(𝑋) ⇒P(𝑋∩𝑌) = 0.75 × 0.54 = 0.405 P( 𝑋∪𝑌 ) = 1 −(0.75 + 0.52 −0.405) = 1 −0.865 = 0.13",
