    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-232-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 9 (12 marks) An ATAR Dance performance consists of 6 dancers. The dancers can be chosen from seven Year 11 students and six Year 12 students. One dancer from each year is also a choreographer. (a) Find the number of ways the dancers can be selected if ; (i) they must consist of equal numbers of Year 11s and Year 12s, (2 marks) (ii) they must consist of at least one dancer from each year, (2 marks) (iii) either the Year 11 choreographer or the Year 12 choreographer must be included, but not both. (2 marks) (iv) there are more Year 11 dancers than Year 12 dancers. (3 marks) CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 9 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-232-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF SN085-232-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF The six dancers are chosen. The choreographer splits the dancers into two groups, 𝑎𝑎 and 𝑏𝑏. (b) Expand the expression (𝑎𝑎+ 𝑏𝑏)6. (1 mark) (c) Explain what the coefficient of the 𝑎𝑎2𝑏𝑏4 term means in the context of the question. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 6 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-232-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 9 (12 marks) An ATAR Dance performance consists of 6 dancers. The dancers can be chosen from seven Year 11 students and six Year 12 students. One dancer from each year is also a choreographer. (a) Find the number of ways the dancers can be selected if (i) they must consist of equal numbers of Year 11s and Year 12s, (2 marks) Solution ✓ (7 3) (6 3) = 700✓ Specific behaviours ✓ Correctly selects from each year. ✓ Determines selections. (ii) they must consist of at least one dancer from each year, (2 marks) Solution ✓ (13 6 ) −(7 0) (6 6) −(7 6) (6 0) = 1708✓ Specific behaviours ✓ Determines selections with no restrictions. ✓ Subtracts selections without 11s or 12s and determines answer. (iii) either the Year 11 choreographer or the Year 12 choreographer must be included, but not both. (2 marks) Solution ✓ (2 1) (11 5 ) = 924✓ Specific behaviours ✓ Selects one choreographer. ✓ Selects the remaining five dancers and determines answer. (iv) there are more Year 11 dancers than Year 12 dancers. (3 marks) Solution ✓ ✓ 7 6 7 6 7 6 4 2 5 1 6 0 35 15 21 6 7 1    +",
