    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-232-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 10 (9 marks) (a) Use the table of approximations shown below and an identity from the standard formula sheet to obtain an approximate value for cos 19°. (3 marks) (b) Peter can see Ray in a dingy, 450 metres away on a bearing of 110°T. Mary, who is 200 metres to the south of Peter, can also see Ray in the dingy. At what bearing must Ray be looking if his gaze is fixed on Mary ? (6 marks) Solution cos 19° = cos(55° −36°) ✓ = cos 55° × cos 36° + sin 55° × sin36° = 4 7 × 4 5 + 5 6 × 3 5 ✓ = 1 5 (16 7 + 5 2) = 1 5 × 32 + 35 14 = 67 70 = 0.9571✓ Specific behaviours ✓ writes angle as a difference ✓ correctly substitutes values from table into relevant identity ✓ correct approximation 𝜃 cos 𝜃 sin 𝜃 36° 4 5 3 5 55° 4 7 5 6 Solution 2 2 200 450 2 200 450 cos(70 ) x = + −    ✓ 425.366 (3 . .) m s f = ✓ 2 2 2 200 425.366 450 2 425.366 450 cos( )   = + −   solve CP 26.22 =  ✓ Bearing = 360 70  − −  263.78 =  Ray must be looking at bearing 264 T  ✓ Specific behaviours ✓ Correct diagram shape and names labelled ✓ Correct placement 70 and 110 degrees , 450m and 20",
