    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 11 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-112-2 Question 16 (9 marks) In a group of 250 students, it is known that 70% are in Year 12 and the rest in Year 11. 40% of those in Year 12 and 80% of those in Year 11 study maths, while the remainder do not. Let 𝑇 be the subset of students in Year 12 and 𝑀 be the subset of students who study maths. (a) Use set language and notation to describe the subset of students who (i) do not study maths. (1 mark) (ii) study maths or are in Year 12. (1 mark) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student from the group (i) is in Year 11 and studies math. (1 mark) (ii) studies math. (2 marks) (iii) is in Year 11, given that they study math. (2 marks) (iv) is in Year 12 or studies math. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 11 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-112-4 Question 16 (9 marks) In a group of 250 students, it is known that 70% are in Year 12 and the rest in Year 11. 40% of those in Year 12 and 80% of those in Year 11 study maths, while the remainder do not. Let 𝑇 be the subset of students in Year 12 and 𝑀 be the subset of students who study maths. (a) Use set language and notation to describe the subset of students who (i) do not study maths. (1 mark) (ii) study maths or are in Year 12. (1 mark) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student from the group (i) is in Year 11 and studies math. (1 mark) (ii) studies math. (2 marks) (iii) is in Year 11, given that they study math. (2 marks) (iv) is in Year 12 or studies math. (2 marks) Solution (i) 𝑀̅ (ii) 𝑀∪𝑇 Specific behaviours  correct notation  correct notation (Penalise use of 𝑃(… ) once) Solution 𝑃(𝑇̅ ∩𝑀) = 0.3 × 0.8 = 0.24 (≡6 25) Specific behaviours  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑇∩𝑀) + 𝑃(𝑇̅ ∩𝑀) = 0.7 × 0.4 + 0.24 = 0.28 + 0.24 = 0.52 (≡13 25) Specific behaviours  indicates suitable method  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑇̅|𝑀) = 0.24 0.52 = 6 13 (≈0.4615) Specific behaviours  uses (i) and (ii)  ex",
