    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 12 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-112-2 Question 17 (9 marks) For two events, 𝑋 and 𝑌, it is known that 𝑃(𝑋) = 0.48 and 𝑃(𝑌) = 0.35. Determine the following probabilities, using any additional information only within that part of the question. (a) 𝑃(𝑌̅). (1 mark) (b) 𝑃(𝑌∩𝑋̅) when 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌̅) = 0.29. (2 marks) (c) 𝑃(𝑋|𝑌) when 𝑃(𝑌|𝑋) = 0.7. (3 marks) (d) 𝑃(𝑋∪𝑌) when 𝑋 and 𝑌 are independent. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 12 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-112-4 Question 17 (9 marks) For two events, 𝑋 and 𝑌, it is known that 𝑃(𝑋) = 0.48 and 𝑃(𝑌) = 0.35. Determine the following probabilities, using any additional information only within that part of the question. (a) 𝑃(𝑌̅). (1 mark) (b) 𝑃(𝑌∩𝑋̅) when 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌̅) = 0.29. (2 marks) (c) 𝑃(𝑋|𝑌) when 𝑃(𝑌|𝑋) = 0.7. (3 marks) (d) 𝑃(𝑋∪𝑌) when 𝑋 and 𝑌 are independent. (3 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝑌̅) = 1 −0.35 = 0.65 Specific behaviours  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌) = 0.48 −0.29 = 0.19 𝑃(𝑌∩𝑋̅) = 0.35 −0.19 = 0.16 Specific behaviours  calculates 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌)  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌) = 0.48 × 0.7 = 0.336 𝑃(𝑋|𝑌) = 0.336 0.35 = 0.96 Specific behaviours  indicates use of conditional probability rule  calculates 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌)  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌) = 0.48 × 0.35 = 0.168 𝑃(𝑋∪𝑌) = 0.48 + 0.35 −0.168 = 0.662 Specific behaviours  uses independence to calculate 𝑃(𝑋∩𝑌)  indicates use of addition rule  correct probability",
