    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "METHODS UNIT 1 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-112-6 Question 19 (12 marks) During 2018, the altitude of the sun, 𝜃 degrees, at noon in Melbourne on the 𝑛𝑡ℎ day of the year can be modelled by the equation 𝜃= 23.5 sin(8𝜋(101 + 𝑛) 1461 ) + 52.2 (a) On the 26th of January, the altitude was 71.4°. Calculate the altitude ten days earlier. (2 marks) (b) Graph the altitude on the axes below for 0 ≤𝑛≤365. (4 marks) CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 15 METHODS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-112-6 (c) State the minimum altitude of the sun at noon in Melbourne and on which day of the year this occurred. (2 marks) (d) Solar panels on the roof of a Melbourne business are designed to meet its entire power needs on cloudless days when the altitude of the sun is at least 36° at noon. (i) Draw a straight line on your graph to represent this requirement. (1 mark) (ii) Determine the number of days the panels are expected to achieve this aim during 2018, ignoring the possibility of cloud cover. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNIT 1 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-112-8 Question 19 (12 marks) During 2018, the altitude of the sun, 𝜃 degrees, at noon in Melbourne on the 𝑛𝑡ℎ day of the year can be modelled by the equation 𝜃= 23.5 sin(8𝜋(101 + 𝑛) 1461 ) + 52.2 (a) On the 26th of January, the altitude was 71.4°. Calculate the altitude ten days earlier. (2 marks) (b) Graph the altitude on the axes below for 0 ≤𝑛≤365. (4 marks) Solution 𝑛= 16, 𝜃= 73.4° Specific behaviours  indicates 𝑛  correct angle Solution See graph Specific behaviours  endpoints, 𝜃≈75°  minimum close to (173, 29°)  maximum close to right endpoint  smooth curve",
