    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 15 METHODS UNITS 1 AND 2 See next page Question 20 (8 marks) A pyramid with a rectangular base of length 𝐿 and width 𝑤 has perpendicular height ℎ. The length of the base is three times its width and the sum of the width, length and height is 180 cm. (a) Calculate the length, height and volume of the pyramid when 𝑤= 15 cm. (2 marks) (b) Show that the volume of the pyramid is given by 𝑉= 180𝑤D −4𝑤C. (2 marks) (c) Use calculus to determine the maximum volume of the pyramid. (4 marks) Solution 𝐿= 3 × 15 = 45, ℎ= 180 −15 −45 = 120 𝑉= 1 3 (15 × 45) × 120 = 27 000 cmC Specific behaviours ü correct length and height ü correct volume Solution 𝑑𝑉 𝑑𝑤= 360𝑤−12𝑤D 360𝑤−12𝑤D = 0 ⇒𝑤= 0, 30 𝑉 29 30 31 𝑑𝑉 𝑑𝑊 + 0 − Sign test indicates 𝑉= 30 is maximum (𝑤= 0 gives zero volume) 𝑉••I = 180(30)D −4(30)C = 54 000 cmC Specific behaviours ü correct derivative using given variables ü solves derivative equal to zero ü uses sign test to show maximum at 𝑤= 30 ü correct maximum volume Solution 𝐿= 3𝑤, ℎ= 180 −𝑤−3𝑤= 180 −4𝑤 𝑉= 1 3 (𝑤× 3𝑤)(180 −4𝑤) = 180𝑤D −4𝑤C Specific behaviours ü expressions for length and height ü substitutes width, length and height correctly",
