    "questionText": "METHODS UNITS 1 AND 2 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN018-142-2 Question 18 (7 marks) A right circular cone of base radius 10 cm and height 25 cm stands on a horizontal surface. A cylinder of radius 𝑥𝑥 cm and volume 𝑉𝑉 cm3 stands inside the cone with its axis coincident with that of the cone and such that the cylinder touches the curved surface of the cone as shown. (a) Show that the volume of the cylinder, 𝑉𝑉= 25𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥2 −2.5𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥3. (3 marks) (b) Given that 𝑥𝑥 can vary, use a calculus method to determine the maximum value of 𝑉𝑉. (4 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNITS 1 AND 2 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN018-142-4 Question 18 (7 marks) A right circular cone of base radius 10 cm and height 25 cm stands on a horizontal surface. A cylinder of radius 𝑥𝑥 cm and volume 𝑉𝑉 cm3 stands inside the cone with its axis coincident with that of the cone and such that the cylinder touches the curved surface of the cone as shown. (a) Show that the volume of the cylinder, 𝑉𝑉= 25𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥2 −2.5𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥3. (3 marks) (b) Given that 𝑥𝑥 can vary, use a calculus method to determine the maximum value of 𝑉𝑉. (4 marks) Solution From similar triangles ℎ 10 −𝑥𝑥= 25 10 ⇒ℎ= 25 −2.5𝑥𝑥 Hence 𝑉𝑉= 𝜋𝜋𝑟𝑟2ℎ 𝑉𝑉= 𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥2(25 −2.5𝑥𝑥) = 25𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥2 −2.5𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥3 Specific behaviours  relation between 𝑥𝑥 and ℎ using similar triangles  expresses ℎ in terms of 𝑥𝑥  substitutes into cylinder volume formula Solution 𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑 𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑= 50𝜋𝜋𝜋𝜋−7.5𝜋𝜋𝑥𝑥2 𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑 𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑= 0 when 𝑥𝑥= 0, 𝑥𝑥= 20 3 𝑥𝑥= 0 ⇒𝑉𝑉= 0 (minimum) 𝑥𝑥= 20 3 ⇒𝑉𝑉= 10 000𝜋𝜋 27 ≈1164 cm3 (maximum) Specific behaviours  derivative  equates derivative to 0  solves for 𝑥𝑥  states maximum volume",
