    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 METHODS UNITS 1&2 See next page SN085-162-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 11 (7 marks) A set of 175 undergraduates were asked to choose their electives for the following year. 85 chose calculus, 58 chose statistics and 67 chose neither calculus nor statistics. (a) Determine how many of the undergraduates chose both calculus and statistics. (2 marks) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen undergraduate from the set chose (i) statistics. (1 mark) (ii) statistics but not calculus. (1 mark) (iii) statistics given that they chose calculus. (1 mark) (c) Use your answers above to explain whether the choice of statistics and calculus electives is independent for these undergraduates. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 METHODS UNITS 1&2 See next page SN085-162-4 Question 11 (7 marks) A set of 175 undergraduates were asked to choose their electives for the following year. 85 chose calculus, 58 chose statistics and 67 chose neither calculus nor statistics. (a) Determine how many of the undergraduates chose both calculus and statistics. (2 marks) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen undergraduate from the set chose (i) statistics. (1 mark) (ii) statistics but not calculus. (1 mark) (iii) statistics given that they chose calculus. (1 mark) (c) Use your answers above to explain whether the choice of statistics and calculus electives is independent for these undergraduates. (2 marks) Solution 𝑛(𝐶∪𝑆) = 175 −67 = 108 𝑛(𝐶∩𝑆) = 85 + 58 −108 = 35 Hence 35 chose both electives. Specific behaviours  indicates union of sets  correct number Solution 𝑃(𝑆) = 58 175 ≈0.3314 Specific behaviours  correct probability Solution Choice is not independent, as 𝑃(𝑆) ≠𝑃(𝑆|𝐶). (Can use other independence conditions) Specific behaviours  states not independent  explanation using existing probabilities Solution 𝑃(𝑆∩𝐶̅) = 58 −35 175 = 23 175 ≈0.1314 Specific behaviours  correct probabi",
