    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "METHODS UNITS 1&2 16 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-162-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 19 (6 marks) A reader bought 15 different novels, planning to read a selection of them when on holiday. (a) Determine the number of different combinations of novels the reader could choose from if they select (i) five novels. (1 mark) (ii) four or five novels. (2 marks) Six of the 15 different novels are by the author Rowe. (b) Determine the number of different combinations the reader could choose from if they select 5 novels of which (i) none of the novels selected are by Rowe. (1 mark) (ii) three of the novels selected are by Rowe. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNITS 1&2 16 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-162-4 Question 19 (6 marks) A reader bought 15 different novels, planning to read a selection of them when on holiday. (a) Determine the number of different combinations of novels the reader could choose from if they select (i) five novels. (1 mark) (ii) four or five novels. (2 marks) Six of the 15 different novels are by the author Rowe. (b) Determine the number of different combinations the reader could choose from if they select 5 novels of which (i) none of the novels selected are by Rowe. (1 mark) (ii) three of the novels selected are by Rowe. (2 marks) Solution (15 5 ) = 3 003 combinations Specific behaviours  correct number Solution (9 2) (6 3) = 36 × 20 = 720 Specific behaviours  one selection correct  correct number Solution Must choose from 9 not by Rowe: (9 5) = 126 Specific behaviours  correct number Solution (15 4 ) + (15 5 ) = 1365 + 3003 = 4 368 combinations Specific behaviours  ways to choose four  correct number",
