    "questionText": "METHODS UNITS 1 AND 2 4 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-102-3 Question 10 (8 marks) A group of 160 students were asked whether they had bought a drink or a snack from the school canteen. 32 had bought neither, 32 had bought both and 112 had bought a drink. (a) Determine the number of students who only bought a snack. (2 marks) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student from the group had bought (i) a drink or a snack. (1 mark) (ii) only a drink. (1 mark) (iii) a drink given that they had bought a snack. (2 marks) (c) For this group of students, are the events buying a snack and buying a drink independent? Justify your answer. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNITS 1 AND 2 4 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-102-4 Question 10 (8 marks) A group of 160 students were asked whether they had bought a drink or a snack from the school canteen. 32 had bought neither, 32 had bought both and 112 had bought a drink. (a) Determine the number of students who only bought a snack. (2 marks) (b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student from the group had bought (i) a drink or a snack. (1 mark) (ii) only a drink. (1 mark) (iii) a drink given that they had bought a snack. (2 marks) (c) For this group of students, are the events buying a snack and buying a drink independent? Justify your answer. (2 marks) Solution 𝑃= 32 32 + 16 = 32 48 = 2 3 Specific behaviours ✓ uses conditional probability ✓ correct probability Solution 𝑛(𝑆∪𝐷) = 160 −32 = 128 𝑛(𝑆∩𝐷̅) = 128 −112 = 16 Specific behaviours ✓ Venn diagram or other method ✓ correct number Solution 𝑃(𝐷) = 112 160 = 7 10 and 𝑃(𝐷|𝑆) = 2 3 and so 𝑃(𝑆) ≠𝑃(𝑆|𝐷) and so events are NOT independent. Specific behaviours ✓ uses probabilities ✓ correct conclusion Solution 𝑃= 128 160 = 4 5 Specific behaviours ✓ correct probability Solution 𝑃= 112 −32 160 = 80 160 = 1 2 Specific behaviours ✓ cor",
