    "topicLabel": "Topic 1.1 · Counting & Probability",
    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "METHODS UNITS 1 AND 2 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-102-3 Question 19 (8 marks) Events 𝐴 and 𝐵 occur at random and it is known that 𝑃(𝐴) = 0.2 and 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 0.68. (a) Determine 𝑃(𝐵) when (i) 𝐴 and 𝐵 are mutually exclusive. (1 mark) (ii) 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵̅) = 0.15. (1 mark) (iii) 𝐴 and 𝐵 are independent. (3 marks) (b) Determine 𝑃(𝐴|𝐵) if 𝑃(𝐴|𝐵̅) = 0.36. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNITS 1 AND 2 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-102-4 Question 19 (8 marks) Events 𝐴 and 𝐵 occur at random and it is known that 𝑃(𝐴) = 0.2 and 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) = 0.68. (a) Determine 𝑃(𝐵) when (i) 𝐴 and 𝐵 are mutually exclusive. (1 mark) (ii) 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵̅) = 0.15. (1 mark) (iii) 𝐴 and 𝐵 are independent. (3 marks) (b) Determine 𝑃(𝐴|𝐵) if 𝑃(𝐴|𝐵̅) = 0.36. (3 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝐵) = 0.68 −0.2 = 0.48 Specific behaviours ✓ states probability Solution Let 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵̅) = 𝑥 and 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵 ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅) = 0.32 𝑥 𝑥+ 0.32 = 0.36 𝑥= 0.18 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 0.2 −0.18 = 0.02 𝑃(𝐴|𝐵) = 0.02 0.48 + 0.02 = 0.02 0.5 = 0.04 Specific behaviours ✓ determines 𝑃(𝐴̅ ∩𝐵) ✓ determines 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) ✓ states probability Solution 𝑃(𝐵) = 𝑃(𝐴∪𝐵) −𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵̅) = 0.68 −0.15 = 0.53 Specific behaviours ✓ uses probability laws ✓ states probability Solution Let 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) = 𝑥 and 𝑃(𝐴) × 𝑃(𝐵) = 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) 0.2 × (0.48 + 𝑥) = 𝑥 𝑥= 0.12 𝑃(𝐵) = 0.48 + 0.12 = 0.6 Specific behaviours ✓ uses probability law for independence ✓ determines 𝑃(𝐴∩𝐵) ✓ states probability",
    "questionImage": "generated/resource-previews/docx-pdf-cache-waep-2017-yr11-meth-u12-s2-c22364a999-p014-question-d4271f207f.jpg",
