    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 9 METHODS UNITS 1&2 See next page SN085-182-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 14 (8 marks) Two events 𝑆 and 𝑇 are such that 𝑃(𝑆) = 0.46 and 𝑃(𝑇) = 0.35. Determine the following probabilities. (a) 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇 ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅) when 𝑆 and 𝑇 are mutually exclusive. (2 marks) (b) 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇) when 𝑃(𝑆̅ ∩𝑇) = 0.22. (2 marks) (c) 𝑃(𝑆∩𝑇̅) when 𝑆 and 𝑇 are independent. (2 marks) (d) 𝑃(𝑇|𝑆) when 𝑃(𝑆|𝑇) = 0.6. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 9 METHODS UNITS 1&2 See next page SN085-182-4 Question 14 (8 marks) Two events 𝑆 and 𝑇 are such that 𝑃(𝑆) = 0.46 and 𝑃(𝑇) = 0.35. Determine the following probabilities. (a) 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇 ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅) when 𝑆 and 𝑇 are mutually exclusive. (2 marks) (b) 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇) when 𝑃(𝑆̅ ∩𝑇) = 0.22. (2 marks) **Parts c) & d) no deduction if rounded to 2 decimal places, just comment (-1 mark once if 1 dp) (c) 𝑃(𝑆∩𝑇̅) when 𝑆 and 𝑇 are independent. (2 marks) (d) 𝑃(𝑇|𝑆) when 𝑃(𝑆|𝑇) = 0.6. (2 marks) Solution 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇) = 𝑃(𝑆) + 𝑃(𝑆̅ ∩𝑇) = 0.46 + 0.22 = 0.68 Specific behaviours  indicates suitable method  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇) = 0.46 + 0.35 = 0.81 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇) = 1 −0.81 = 0.19 Specific behaviours  indicates 𝑃(𝑆∪𝑇)  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑆∩𝑇) = 0.46 × 0.35 = 0.161 𝑃(𝑆∩𝑇̅) = 0.46 −0.161 = 0.299 Specific behaviours  indicates 𝑃(𝑆∩𝑇)  correct probability Solution 𝑃(𝑆∩𝑇) = 0.35 × 0.6 = 0.21 𝑃(𝑇|𝑆) = 0.21 ÷ 0.46 = 21 46 ≈0.457 Specific behaviours  indicates 𝑃(𝑆∩𝑇)  correct probability",
