    "questionText": "METHODS UNITS 1&2 10 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-182-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 15 (5 marks) Consider the graph below: (a) Determine the average rate of change of the function 𝑦= 𝑓(𝑥) between 𝑥= 2 and 𝑥= 4. (2 marks) The following table shows points on the curve from the graph above of 𝑦= 𝑓(𝑥) . 𝑥 2 2.01 2.1 3 𝑦 1.2 1.216 1.37 3.6 (b) Use all the information in the table above to demonstrate how to use the difference quotient lim ℎ→0 𝑓(𝑥+ ℎ) −𝑓(𝑥) ℎ to show that the instantaneous rate of change of the function 𝑦= 𝑓(𝑥) at 𝑥 = 2 is 1.6. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "METHODS UNITS 1&2 10 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-182-4 Question 15 (5 marks) (a) Determine the average rate of change of the function 𝑦= 𝑓(𝑥) between 𝑥= 2 and 𝑥= 4. (2 marks) Solution 𝐴𝑅𝐶= 8 −1.2 4 −2 = 3.4 Specific behaviours  uses correct idea  correct rate The following table shows points on the curve. 𝑥 3 2.1 2.01 𝑦 3.6 1.37 1.216 (b) Use all the information in the table above to demonstrate how to use the difference quotient lim ℎ→0 𝑓(𝑥+ ℎ) −𝑓(𝑥) ℎ to show that the instantaneous rate of change of the function 𝑦= 𝑓(𝑥) at 𝑥 = 2 is 1.6. (3 marks) Solution 𝑓(3) −𝑓(2) 1 = 3.6 −1.2 1 = 2.4  𝑓(2.1) −𝑓(2) 0.1 = 1.37 −1.2 0.1 = 1.7 𝑓(2.01) −𝑓(2) 0.01 = 1.216 −1.2 0.01 = 1.6 Specific behaviours See above",
